# Day 45

**31km / 9 hours**

*Tongariro to Waihohonu Hut*

This was slated to be one of the most spectacular and stunning days of the whole walk, coming round the incredible Mount Tongariro and seeing the awesome volcanic features. Given that, it shouldn't be a surprise that it was cold and wet all day; the clouds obscuring most views that there might be to see. The rain which was often heavy also made it difficult to take the phone out to take photos.

![The cloudy view](./clouds.avif)

That being said there was still some awesome things to see,  notably one of the emerald lakes which the track travelled close enough to see. Also just generally the volcanic landscape is spectacular and a totally different area to the normal hiking environment. I'm not a geologist but the rocks are very cool to see, the ground and plants are also fascinating in their differences from general.

![An emerald lake blowing profusely in the heavy wind up on the volcano](./emerald-lake.avif)

![The landscape from afar](./volcanic-landscape.avif)

![Another landscape. Can you spot the hut in the picture?](./landscape-hut.avif)

The day began with a few kilometres on the road to get to the carpark to begin the actual track which curved upwards through the bush and alongside a nice stream. The track was very well maintained and going upwards was hard work and made it quite hot which was to be inconvenient later. After leaving the bush line it was still stairs but it gradually got colder, wetter and windier the higher up we got. Despite the adverse conditions there were a surprising number of day walkers making their way the opposite direction with various levels of preparedness. 

Eventually we made it up to the top of the track at over 1,700 metres above sea level with a junction where we took the Northern Circuit track rather than the Alpine crossing which used to be the official trail. At this point I had put on many layers of clothing which was tricky to do in the wind and rain and was reasonably warm despite the chill. From there it was coming down and away from those heights the clouds were less rampant so it was more comfortable walking conditions. I could also see further and it was generally nicer. There is a tinge of regret that the poor weather made it less spectacular but that's the nature of tramping. 

This track meandered along, dropping eventually to the hut at around 1,100 metres above sea level which was a huge relief since it was a mentally exhausting day. Not sure where to go tomorrow, might depend on the weather and conditions.